/T (One-third \(31%\) of the household population in Ethiopia has access to at least basic sanitation service, meaning they use improved facilities that are not shared with other households or have safely managed sanitation service where excreta are disposed of in situ or transported and treated off-site. Twenty-eight percent of the population uses unimproved sanitation facilities, 14% has limited sanitation service, and 27% practices open defecation. Access to basic sanitation services is slightly higher in urban areas \(35%\) than rural areas \(30%\).)
/E (One-third \(31%\) of the household population in Ethiopia has access to at least basic sanitation service, meaning they use improved facilities that are not shared with other households or have safely managed sanitation service where excreta are disposed of in situ or transported and treated off-site. Twenty-eight percent of the population uses unimproved sanitation facilities, 14% has limited sanitation service, and 27% practices open defecation. Access to basic sanitation services is slightly higher in urban areas \(35%\) than rural areas \(30%\).)
